<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Sprint 089 C3 — minimal entitlements plist. Apple: "Don't
      include an entitlement if the value is false." Start with only
      what's demonstrably required.

      allow-jit is required for V8's TurboFan JIT compiler that
      Chromium (Electron's renderer) uses.

      The following exceptions are deliberately absent for the first
      pass and added back only if a specific runtime failure demands
      them (Sprint 088 carried them for stale reasons):

      - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      - com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
      - com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory

      Every .so extension module in the bundled Python runtime is
      re-signed with our Developer ID identity by electron-builder's
      osx-sign walk, so library-validation should pass without the
      exception. If a specific .so still fails to load, investigate
      that specific file rather than lower the app-wide guard.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Chromium's renderer process allocates writable-then-executable
         memory for V8's JIT. allow-jit alone is insufficient on modern
         macOS; the renderer helper also requires allow-unsigned-
         executable-memory. Without it, Substrate Helper (Renderer) is
         killed by the sandbox at launch with a tccd.system / lsd.modifydb
         denial and the whole .app dies with no window. Verified on
         macOS 26 arm64, 2026-09-28. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Network access — the daemon binds 127.0.0.1 and talks to
         Ollama, Anthropic, OpenAI. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
